                    <run bold='true' fontcolor='#1f6f8b' fontsize='14'>Key findings</run>
                    <run>Æ&#10;</run>
                    <run fontcolor='#1d2b3a' fontsize='12'>• </run>
                    <run bold='true' fontcolor='#1d2b3a' fontsize='12'>25,466 loans from 2025</run>
                    <run fontcolor='#1d2b3a' fontsize='12'> are too new for a 12-month result, but the model can already score them.</run>
                    <run>Æ&#10;</run>
                    <run fontcolor='#1d2b3a' fontsize='12'>• </run>
                    <run bold='true' fontcolor='#1d2b3a' fontsize='12'>3,131 (12%) are in the riskiest group.</run>
                    <run fontcolor='#1d2b3a' fontsize='12'> If they behave like past riskiest loans (24.5% default), about 770 will default.</run>
                    <run>Æ&#10;</run>
                    <run fontcolor='#1d2b3a' fontsize='12'>• </run>
                    <run bold='true' fontcolor='#1d2b3a' fontsize='12'>Concentrated in Denmark and Latvia:</run>
                    <run fontcolor='#1d2b3a' fontsize='12'> about 81% of young loans there are in the riskiest group, vs 10% in Finland and Estonia and 0% in the Netherlands.</run>
                    <run>Æ&#10;</run>
